 but cosmetic elegance is important a  little doesn't go a long way with  sunscreens  for a sunscreen to work you need to be  applying the right amount which is quite  a lot  and reapplying it often and you're less  likely to do that with a sunscreen  that's super white and super thick  a cerave survey found that 55 percent of  people who skipped daily sunscreen  did this because they didn't like the  way it feels during and after  application  38 said that white cast was an issue  there's also a study from  a sunscreen ingredient manufacturer so  flagging for possible bias but they make  both organic and inorganic sunscreen  ingredients  they found that people unconsciously  used 29 less of a 6  titanium dioxide sunscreen versus a  sunscreen that had 3  titanium dioxide and 3 of a chemical  sunscreen ingredient  you can also see a lot of reviews of  zinc oxide sunscreens online where they  say  it's really thick but a little goes a  long way which  just doesn't work the other big issue we  don't really talk about much with  mineral sunscreens  is stability and not photo stability but  just  regular stability for a sunscreen to  work well the sunscreen ingredients have  to stay spread out in the sunscreen  in the bottle during its lifetime plus  when you're applying it on your skin  plus when it's been sitting on your skin  for a while if it clumps up then you get  holes in your protection  most chemical sunscreens are soluble  which means it's mixed at a molecular  level  it's a little bit like vinegar when you  have vinegar the vinegar molecule stays  spread out in the vinegar for a really  long time  they don't eventually sink to the bottom  but when you have bigger particles it's  a lot harder to keep them separated for  longer you can use  a thicker texture with rheology  modifiers to keep the particles further  apart for longer  you can also coat the particles to try  to stop them from clumping  but at the end of the day particles are  always going to be harder to stabilize  than a solution or a normal emulsion  this is especially the case when you  have zinc oxide and titanium dioxide  they are really dense materials four to  six times denser than water  and so gravity wants to pull them to the  bottom and it's even worse when you have  uncoated zinc oxide which a lot of  natural sunscreen companies tend to use  because the coatings tend to be  synthetic zinc oxide itself is already  pretty synthetic but  they just don't use them these encoded  particles really want to clump up a lot  so these large particles tend to clump  up together and separate in the bottle  if you leave it for too long if you let  it get too hot  or if you apply it to your skin and let  it get wet and i think this is the  reason why a lot of mineral sunscreens  especially those from natural brands  that use uncoated zinc oxide  in a really oily base tend to get really  bad scores in consumer reports tests  they measure the spf after something  that's pretty similar to the water  resistance test  they apply the sunscreen to the person  they get the person to sit in water and  then they test the spf  and there are also lots of reviews of  these sorts of products from people who  got burnt while using them and i think  it's a mixture of people being more  likely to under apply  a really thick product and just the  unstable drama queen nature of  uncoated zinc oxide but i think if  you're aware of these issues then it's  fine because you can work around them  make sure you still apply the right  amount of sunscreen don't use it if it's  expired don't let the bottle get too hot  don't use it if it's separated so at the  end of the day is the same thing that i  keep on saying the best sunscreen is the  one that you use  if it's something you enjoy if it's  something that fits in your budget if  it's something that you can easily  access if it's something that doesn't  irritate your skin then it's going to be  the best sunscreen for you  it doesn't really matter if it's  physical or chemical and also remember  sunscreen is not the be-all and end-all  you can also use  other sun protective measures like hats  and clothing so hopefully i've covered  most of the myths about zinc oxide being  more protective if i didn't leave me a  comment with the extra myths  if you liked the video click the like  and the subscribe you can also follow me  on instagram and check out my blog  see you next time hopefully it's not  about sunscreen again but  i can't make any promises because it is  heading to sunscreen season  and you are going to see myths  everywhere
 

